+# Fixes and full rerun, 18 November 2026
+
+**Reference for every number:** the console output you ran yourself and pasted into `improvemt_document.docx` ("the document"). The `results/` folder of the uploaded package is treated as another output to check, not as a source. Everything was rerun from scratch here (Linux, Python 3.13.18, NumPy 2.5.3, SciPy 1.18.1); the rerun is in `verification/full_rerun_2026-11-18/` and is compared line by line with the document in `verification/README.md`.
+
+## 1. What was wrong and what changed
+
+| # | Issue | Where | Label | Fix | Effect |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| F1 | The "method vs network" ratio divided a raw-gradient MSE (0.125) by the paper's filtered-gradient MSE (1.07e-4) and was quoted as 1,160× | `SUMMARY.md` §5, `reports/part3_gaps.md`; `exp_surrogate_truth.py` stored the paper's value next to a raw-basis MSE | Confirmed analysis bug | `exp_surrogate_truth.py` computes both quantities on both bases (`sensitivity_bases`) and checks that the authors' saved `dc0` is the filtered sensitivity (difference 2.2e-12; 0.89 from the raw one) | **601×** in MSE on the paper's filtered basis, 444× on raw gradients; 19× and 17× in relative L2 |
+| F2 | The authors' designs were scored with hole sides below 0.01 clamped to a 1 % slit | `exp_surrogate_truth.py` (the reference spline clamps outside 0.01–0.99) | Methodological ambiguity | Both conventions are reported; `nnto/scoring.py::SmallHolesSolid` counts such holes as absent, as the authors' voxel data does | Authors' designs are **8.5–9.7 %** (holes < 1 % solid) or 9.2–10.3 % (thin slits) worse than reported |
+| F3 | The mean of signed model errors hid cancellation (+0.03, 0.00, −0.08 % averaged to −0.02 %) | summaries in `exp_newer_models_to.py`, `analyse_newer_models.py` | Confirmed reporting bug | Summaries add the mean and largest absolute error (`error_summary`) | Improved network: mean absolute 0.035 %, worst 0.079 %; vs 7.8–8.4 % for the authors' best design: **99–238× smaller**, not "> 300×" |
+| F4 | Published-network runs stop at the 3,000-iteration cap unconverged and 0.01–0.3 % short of the volume target | `exp_newer_models_to.py` | Methodological limitation | Each run records its volume violation and the true compliance at the exact volume target (`project_volume`); `--maxiter` option; new `exp_published_long.py` follows the published network for 30,000 iterations | See §3: the gap to the improved network depends on the iteration budget |
+| F5 | Ground-truth scripts ignored the results-folder setting; `analyse_patch_check.py` crashed on an incomplete `patch_check.npz` (the uploaded one held 2 of 19 runs) | `gt_*.py`, `analyse_patch_check.py` | Confirmed implementation bugs | `NNTO_RESULTS` honoured everywhere (`common.py`, `gt_*.py`), `NNTO_FIGURES` for figures; the analysis skips missing configurations | Reruns can go to a separate folder |
+| F6 | Authors' training set given as 10,000 samples | `SUMMARY.md` §10 | Documentation error | 7,000 training rows of 10,000 (15 % validation, 15 % test) | — |
+
+New files: `nnto/scoring.py`, `experiments/rescore_final_designs.py` (re-scores every saved final design; `results/final_designs_rescored.json`), `experiments/exp_published_long.py`, `tests/test_scoring.py` (7 tests; 107 pass). `make_figures.py` Fig. 4 now shows both hole conventions (bar: solid, whisker: slit).
+
+## 2. Reproducibility (details in `verification/README.md`)
+
+* Every script was rerun from scratch with the fixed code into `verification/full_rerun_2026-11-18/` (about 2 h on 2 cores), except the long density-problem ensembles (`exp_dbto_ensemble.py`, `exp_dbto_part2.py`), the trust-constr multiscale runs (`exp_hbto_runs.py`, never run to completion by design) and the TensorFlow cross-check.
+* Deterministic results reproduce to round-off: ground truth, surrogate truth (≤ 2.3e-14 against the document), all 60 newer networks (bit-identical), all 31 multiscale optimisation runs (identical).
+* An independent finite-element re-implementation (written separately from the package) reproduces the start-design compliances, the authors' saved start-design sensitivities, and every true compliance of the authors' designs and of the optimisation runs.
+* Not bit-reproducible across machines: round-off-sized numbers, the 1-D architecture study (training differs between Mac and Linux), the chaotic density-problem runs, and timings. Conclusions are unchanged.
+* `results/` keeps the uploaded files, except: `surrogate_truth.json` (rerun with the fixed script; all earlier values unchanged to 7.6e-11), `patch_check.json`/`.npz` (the uploaded files held 2 of 19 runs from an interrupted run; replaced by the complete rerun, which matches the uploaded `patch_check_summary.json`), and the new `final_designs_rescored.json` and `published_long.json`. `dbto_ensemble.npz` (1 run) is also left over from an interrupted run; `make_figures.py` uses `dbto_ensemble_part1.npz`.
+
+## 3. Final numbers
+
+See `COMPARISON_WITH_PAPER.md` for the full comparison with the paper. In short:
+* The paper's printed numbers (Table 1 errors, Figs. 8 and 11) come out of the authors' saved models and designs exactly; NNJ, CSM and AD agree to about 1e-15.
+* The paper's CFD discrepancy is a float32 bug (error 1.65e-2 → 2.3e-10 in float64), and its multiscale compliances are 8.5–10.3 % too optimistic.
+* The improved network gives designs with 3.9–4.6 % lower true compliance than the paper's, within 0.1 % of the reference optimum. Against the paper's network with the same optimizer the gain is 3.8 % at 3,000 iterations and about 2 % once that network has plateaued (≈ 80.5 from 10,000 to 30,000 iterations, never converging).
+* Density problem: no measurable gain; repeated runs of one method land 1.5 % apart.
